Reduced repro:

```csharp
new C<Foo<string>>().ToString();

static Type GetObject() => typeof(Foo<object>);
var s = typeof(C<>).MakeGenericType(GetObject()).GetMethod("Set").CreateDelegate<Set<Foo<object>>>();

Foo<object> ob = default;
s(ref ob);
Console.WriteLine(ob.Cookie1);
Console.WriteLine(ob.Cookie2);


delegate void Set<T>(ref T t);

interface IFoo
{
    void Do<T>();
}

struct Foo<T> : IFoo
{
    public nint Cookie1;
    public nint Cookie2;

    public void Do<T1>()
    {
        Cookie1 = 42;
    }
}

class C<T> where T : IFoo
{
    public static void Set(ref T t)
    {
        t.Do<T>();
    }
}
```

This is supposed to print 42 0, instead it prints 0 42.

This is because we messed up the constrained call in `Set`. The bug is in building the generic dictionary of `Set`. Instead of creating a fat pointer that points to the `Do` method, it creates a fat pointer that points to the unboxing stub of the Do method. We end up unboxing something that wasn't boxed.

This happens when the runtime type loader creates the dictionary - so the repro either needs some `MakeGeneric` or a GVM call to land in an instantiation of `C` that we haven't seen at compile time (the dictionary building in the compiler doesn't have this bug).

I haven't decided _where_ the bug is. Some weird things are happening:

* When we're creating the generic dictionary cell, we know the correct address of the non-unboxing method. If I set a breakpoint here:
  https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/3482997ad19498a4887bf61a93d98348548aa9a9/src/coreclr/nativeaot/System.Private.TypeLoader/src/Internal/Runtime/TypeLoader/GenericDictionaryCell.cs#L351-L354

  I can see that the `MethodDesc` we have was parsed properly and we have a good function pointer stored in it. Then we enter `builder.PrepareMethod`.
* As part of preparing the method, we try to locate a template for the method and as part of that, refresh the function pointer with what we found:
  https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/3482997ad19498a4887bf61a93d98348548aa9a9/src/coreclr/nativeaot/System.Private.TypeLoader/src/Internal/Runtime/TypeLoader/TypeBuilder.cs#L314-L317

  This is where things get messed up. The function pointer we looked up was for the unboxing thunk but it's very deliberately masqueraded as not that:

  https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/3482997ad19498a4887bf61a93d98348548aa9a9/src/coreclr/tools/aot/ILCompiler.Compiler/Compiler/DependencyAnalysis/NativeLayoutVertexNode.cs#L756-L758

At minimum, it doesn't look right we allow `SetFunctionPointer` to stomp whatever pointer we already had. It feels like there should be an assert that we either don't have a pointer, or the pointer is equal to what we're trying to set.

